% ============================================================================
% animal-habitat — each animal and the name of the biome it lives in
% (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling fact to `animal-homes.adj`, but a DIFFERENT axis: not the built
% STRUCTURE an animal lives in (a hive, a nest, a burrow), but the broad
% ENVIRONMENT TYPE — its biome — that the animal calls home. "A polar bear
% lives in the arctic; a giraffe lives on the grassland." Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? animal_habitat(polar_bear, $Biome)   % arctic
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `animal_habitat(animal, biome)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% biome's name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on an animal (or a non-animal) that is not in the table.
%
% Each animal and the name of its biome, as a truth table:
%
%     animal           biome        a beginner's cue
%     --------------   ----------   ----------------------------------------
%     polar_bear       arctic       polar bears live on Arctic shores and sea ice
%     bactrian_camel   desert       Bactrian camels live in the rocky deserts of Asia
%     giraffe          grassland    a giraffe's habitat is the African grassland
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind the biome and recall which animal:
%
%     ? animal_habitat($Animal, desert)   % bactrian_camel
%
% Note that a word that is NOT one of these three animals — `dog`, `shark`,
% or a made-up animal — is deliberately NOT a row, so a habitat recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a biome. That honest-abstention
% property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers
% exactly the pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% NOT A DUPLICATE of `animal-homes.adj`: that table names the physical
% STRUCTURE an animal builds or uses (bee->hive, bird->nest, spider->web,
% rabbit->burrow, beaver->lodge) — a different column semantic and a
% disjoint set of example animals from the BIOME/environment-type axis this
% table names. Confirmed via a full-tree grep of adj-facts-stdlib/ for
% "habitat"/"biome" before writing this file (see
% ../README.md's "Known overlapping tables" section for the standing
% discipline this check follows).
%
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (byte-quotable, per the ADJ-facts standard).
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Every row is quoted verbatim from National Geographic (education.
% nationalgeographic.org / kids.nationalgeographic.com) — hence `trust
% consensus` (NOT `authoritative`, which is reserved for a primary/official
% .gov/.edu or standards source), the same tier this stdlib already reserves
% for National Geographic Education's rainforest-layer.adj citation. Nothing
% here is asserted from memory. Because ADJ tables carry ONE shared
% provenance envelope (per-row provenance is a documented future extension —
% see ADJ-TABLES §6), the envelope below cites the polar bear fact page at
% its `locator`, and the `source` quotes that page's habitat sentence
% verbatim. For full auditability, here is the exact span that grounds EACH
% row, copied character-for-character, with the page it was read from:
%
%   polar_bear → arctic     — "Polar bears live along shores and on sea ice
%                              in the icy cold Arctic."
%                              https://kids.nationalgeographic.com/animals/mammals/facts/polar-bear
%
%   bactrian_camel → desert — "Bactrian camels live in Central and East
%                              Asia's rocky deserts where it gets very cold
%                              in the winter and hot in the summer."
%                              https://kids.nationalgeographic.com/animals/mammals/facts/bactrian-camel
%
%   giraffe → grassland     — "Grassland, for example, is the habitat of the
%                              giraffe, but the animal's range is central,
%                              eastern and southern Africa."
%                              https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/habitat
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table animal_habitat {
    columns animal, biome

    row (polar_bear, arctic)
    row (bactrian_camel, desert)
    row (giraffe, grassland)

    source "Polar bears live along shores and on sea ice in the icy cold Arctic."
    locator "https://kids.nationalgeographic.com/animals/mammals/facts/polar-bear"
    trust consensus
}
